===========================================================================
DCE Threads Compatibility Library for Linux
A DCE threads emulation layer on top of POSIX threads (glibc NPTL).
===========================================================================
Maintainers:	Miroslaw Dobrzanski-Neumann <mirek-dn@freenet.de>
		Wez Furlong                 <wez+dce@thebrainroom.com>

The OSF DCE code expects DCE threads: the pthreads draft 4 interface with
DCE exceptions (TRY/CATCH/FINALLY, pthread_cancel_e) and DCE cancel
semantics (general and asynchronous cancelability, a canceled thread
catches the exception and continues).  This library provides them on top
of the system's POSIX threads.  It does not replace the system pthread
functions; the draft 4 functions have their own names (pthd4_*, and
pthd4exc_* for the variants that raise exceptions).  The RPC runtime
calls them through the sys_pthread_* names defined in
include/dce/pthreads_rename.h.

Cancels are implemented by the library itself, because NPTL does not let
a thread continue after it has acted on a cancel.  The environment
variable DCETHREADS_CANCEL=nptl selects the older emulation on top of
NPTL pthread_cancel() instead (for comparison only).

A cancel interrupts a thread that waits in a system call (select, poll,
read, ...) with a real-time signal, SIGRTMAX-2 (signal 62 on Linux) by
default.  The signal is kept blocked in the threads the library knows and
only unblocked atomically while they wait (ppoll/pselect), so a wake-up
cannot get lost; the application must not use this signal itself.  The
environment variable DCETHREADS_WAKE_SIG=<number> selects another
real-time signal (SIGRTMIN..SIGRTMAX; other values keep the default).
In gdb, "handle SIG62 nostop noprint pass" keeps gdb from stopping at
every cancel.

Build and test: see the README in the top directory ("make check" runs
the tests in tests/).

For information about copyrights, see the file `COPYING' included with this
package.

Reference for the DCE threads interface: "Guide to DECthreads" (OpenVMS
documentation), e.g. https://www0.mi.infn.it/~calcolo/OpenVMS/ssb71/6493/6493p001.htm
